# Comment Formatting Rules

## Multiline Comment Requirements

### When to Use Multiline Comments
- **Always use multiline comments** (`/** */`) for complex explanations, documentation, and detailed descriptions
- **Use multiline comments** before method implementations, class definitions, and complex logic blocks
- **Use multiline comments** for explaining business logic, algorithms, and non-obvious code behavior
- **Use multiline comments** for API documentation, parameter descriptions, and return value explanations

### Multiline Comment Format
```java
/**
 * This method calculates the total hours worked for a timesheet.
 * It processes all time logs and applies business rules for overtime calculation.
 * 
 * @param timesheetId The unique identifier of the timesheet
 * @param includeOvertime Whether to include overtime hours in the calculation
 * @return TotalHoursDto containing regular and overtime hours
 * @throws ResourceNotFoundException if timesheet is not found
 */
public TotalHoursDto calculateTotalHours(Long timesheetId, boolean includeOvertime) {
    // Implementation here
}
```

### Multiline Comment Placement
- **Place multiline comments BEFORE** the code they describe
- **Never place multiline comments AFTER** code lines
- **Use multiline comments** to explain the "why" and "how" of complex logic

## Single-Line Comment Restrictions

### Prohibited Single-Line Comment Usage
- **NEVER use single-line comments** (`//`) at the end of code lines
- **NEVER use single-line comments** in between code statements within the same method
- **NEVER use single-line comments** to explain obvious code behavior

### Examples of Prohibited Comments
```java
// ❌ WRONG - Single-line comment at end of line
String name = "John"; // This is the user's name

// ❌ WRONG - Single-line comment between statements
int count = 0;
// Initialize the counter
count++;

// ❌ WRONG - Obvious comment
int age = 25; // Set age to 25
```

### Allowed Single-Line Comment Usage
- **Use single-line comments** only for section headers and brief explanations at the beginning of code blocks
- **Use single-line comments** for TODO, FIXME, and NOTE markers
- **Use single-line comments** for debugging purposes (temporary)

### Examples of Allowed Comments
```java
// TODO: Implement caching mechanism for better performance
// FIXME: Handle edge case when user has no permissions
// NOTE: This method is called from the scheduler every 5 minutes

// Section: Data validation
if (input == null) {
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input cannot be null");
}

// Section: Business logic processing
/**
 * Process the timesheet data according to company policies.
 * This includes calculating regular hours, overtime, and applying
 * any applicable rules or exceptions.
 */
public void processTimesheet(TimesheetDto timesheet) {
    // Implementation here
}
```

## Comment Content Guidelines

### What to Document
- **Business logic** and complex algorithms
- **API contracts** and method signatures
- **Configuration** and setup requirements
- **Performance considerations** and optimizations
- **Security implications** and access control
- **Integration points** with external systems
- **Error handling** strategies and edge cases

### What NOT to Document
- **Obvious code behavior** (e.g., "set variable to 5")
- **Self-explanatory method names** (e.g., `calculateTotal()` doesn't need "calculates the total")
- **Standard library usage** (e.g., "create a new ArrayList")
- **Simple variable assignments** (e.g., "assign value to variable")

## Comment Quality Standards

### Good Comment Examples
```java
/**
 * Validates timesheet entries against company overtime policies.
 * Checks for daily overtime (over 8 hours) and weekly overtime (over 40 hours).
 * Applies different rate calculations based on the overtime type.
 * 
 * @param timeLogs List of time log entries to validate
 * @return ValidationResult containing any policy violations
 */
public ValidationResult validateOvertimePolicies(List<TimeLog> timeLogs) {
    // Implementation here
}

/**
 * Processes bulk timesheet approvals with optimized database queries.
 * Uses batch processing to reduce database round trips and improve performance.
 * Handles partial failures gracefully by rolling back only failed transactions.
 * 
 * @param approvalRequests List of timesheet approval requests
 * @return BulkApprovalResult with success/failure status for each request
 */
public BulkApprovalResult processBulkApprovals(List<ApprovalRequest> approvalRequests) {
    // Implementation here
}
```

### Bad Comment Examples
```java
// ❌ WRONG - Obvious and unhelpful
public void saveData(String data) {
    // Save the data to database
    repository.save(data);
}

// ❌ WRONG - Commenting the obvious
int count = 0; // Initialize count to zero
count++; // Increment count by one

// ❌ WRONG - Single-line comment at end of line
String name = getUserName(); // Get user name from database
```

## Implementation Guidelines

### For New Code
- **Always write multiline comments** before implementing complex methods
- **Document the business requirements** and expected behavior
- **Explain any non-obvious algorithms** or calculations
- **Include parameter and return value descriptions**

### For Existing Code
- **Replace single-line comments** at line endings with proper multiline documentation
- **Add missing documentation** for public APIs and complex methods
- **Remove obvious or redundant comments** that don't add value
- **Update comments** when business logic changes

### For Refactoring
- **Update comments** to reflect the new implementation
- **Remove outdated comments** that no longer apply
- **Add comments** for any new complexity introduced
- **Ensure comments** explain the "why" not just the "what"

## Enforcement

### Code Review Checklist
- [ ] No single-line comments at the end of code lines
- [ ] No single-line comments between statements in the same method
- [ ] All public methods have proper multiline documentation
- [ ] Complex logic is explained with multiline comments
- [ ] Comments explain business logic, not obvious code behavior
- [ ] TODO/FIXME comments are properly formatted and justified

### Automated Checks
- Use IDE settings to highlight single-line comments at line endings
- Configure linting rules to flag prohibited comment patterns
- Include comment quality in code review templates
- Use static analysis tools to identify missing documentation

